{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And you shall not make a tattoo-mark.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: a mark that is etched and fastened so that it is never erased — they prick it with a needle and it remains black permanently — <i>coadura</i> in the vernacular. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in Torat Kohanim: 'One might think even if he wrote but did not tattoo, he would be liable — Scripture says, קעקע (tattoo). If קעקע — one might think even if he tattooed but did not write — Scripture says, וכתבת (and you shall write). How so? He is not liable until he writes and tattoos with ink or with kohl or with anything that leaves a mark. R. Shimon ben Yochai says: he is not liable until he writes the Name of HaShem, as it says: ״וכתבת קעקע לא תתנו בכם אני ה׳״ — that is, you shall not put upon yourselves My Name, which is HaShem.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:09.279198248Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}